Adding and subtracting the exponential series recovers the trigonometric ones, so for every $z in CC$,

$ ee^(ii z) = cos z + ii sin z. $

For real $x$, comparing real and imaginary parts gives $re(ee^(ii x)) = cos x$ and $im(ee^(ii x)) = sin x$. Taking $x = ppi$ yields Euler's identity $ee^(ii ppi) = -1$.
